 ## Overview
-Tetrahedral Strips and Fans are specialized graphics primitives designed to address the bandwidth bottleneck between main memory and the graphics processor when rendering unstructured tetrahedral meshes [S1, unofficial][S2, unofficial]. They reduce the number of vertices required to transmit tetrahedra, achieving performance improvements similar to triangle strips and fans for triangle meshes [S1, unofficial][S2, unofficial]. Unlike triangle strips, which can be statically computed, tetrahedral decomposition is view-dependent, preventing the use of static strips or display lists when changing viewpoints [S2, unofficial]. These primitives enable order-independent transparency and volume scan conversion to be handled by graphics hardware, making a software sorting pass unnecessary [S1, unofficial].
+Tetrahedral Strips and Fans are specialized graphics primitives designed to address the bandwidth bottleneck between main memory and the graphics processor when rendering unstructured tetrahedral meshes [S2, unofficial]. They reduce the number of vertices required to transmit tetrahedra, achieving performance improvements similar to triangle strips and fans for triangle meshes [S1, unofficial][S2, unofficial]. Unlike triangle strips, which can be statically computed, the decomposition of tetrahedra into triangles is view-dependent, preventing the use of static strips or display lists when changing viewpoints [S2, unofficial]. These primitives enable order-independent transparency and volume scan conversion to be handled by graphics hardware, making a software sorting pass unnecessary [S1, unofficial].
 
